WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.897 --> 00:00:07.370
So, there's five steps to the creative process
that kinda spans all across the board, 

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:10.261
whether you're an architect or a writer.

00:00:10.261 --> 00:00:14.819
So, let me just start off by saying 
that there are no new ideas.

00:00:14.819 --> 00:00:20.381
The creative process is the act of taking old ideas
and creating new connections between them

00:00:20.381 --> 00:00:22.595
or seeing them in a new light.

00:00:22.595 --> 00:00:24.940
You're never starting from a blank slate.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:26.980
In fact, that would be kinda terrifying.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.016
You're taking existing constraints,
you're taking old ideas,

00:00:31.016 --> 00:00:32.300
and you're just combining them

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.321
in a way that's never been done before.

00:00:35.321 --> 00:00:38.350
So, in his book, "A Technique
for Producing Ideas",

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.770
James Webb Young describes
the five processes

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.830
and I've just added a
couple of my own,

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:47.550
and so the first step, is gathering.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.900
You're gathering all the data,

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.680
all the information there
is to know about your topic,

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.700
about areas around your topic,

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.220
and also learning about
concepts from other areas

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:02.930
that might relate to your topic.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.520
For example, when I was
doing research for my thesis,

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.180
which was a library,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.860
I was researching about
the history of books,

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.230
the cognitive psychology behind reading,

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:19.090
monasteries, the Parisian arcades,

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:23.630
and then collecting all
the data around libraries

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:27.000
that exist now, and
that has existed before,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.020
and doing a very thorough case study.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.250
And the second step, is processing.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:34.850
That's when you look at all
the data you've gathered

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.290
and you try to see how you can fit them

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.060
into different angles

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.470
and combine them in ways that
hasn't been combined before.

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:46.090
And the third step, is you try
to come up with a solution.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.440
At a certain point, you
have to stop researching

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.740
and you have to start designing.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.640
Try to come up with solutions.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:57.330
Come up with five solutions,
come up with 10 solutions.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.500
The more, the better.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.050
The more solutions you
try to come up with,

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:05.740
you'll be able to see what
works and what doesn't work.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.260
You have to think by doing.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.880
And number four, take a walk.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.290
Stop working on the
problem for a little while

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.760
and go and do something
else that interests you

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.960
or something that's mentally stimulating.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:23.510
You could go to an art gallery,

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.240
you could go for a run, you
could go to a film festival.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.000
Number five, let your idea simmer

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.850
and let it come back to you.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.250
After some time,

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:39.250
it's gonna come back in a new
form, attached to other ideas.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.140
If you're really focused

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.120
on trying to solve this one problem,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.020
no matter what you're doing,

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.490
as you're going about your daily life,

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:51.410
everything will inevitably

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:55.550
gravitate around that,
and it'll feed that.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.490
Even if you try your best not to,

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:01.570
the world is just so full of connections.

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:05.220
And after doing that much
research into something,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.240
your idea is gonna pull other ideas

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.570
and they'll latch on to create new ideas.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:14.290
So many inventions throughout history

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:17.560
have happened not through
isolated studying,

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.380
but by mingling with other ideas,

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.060
and actually, a lot of times, by accident.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.840
In some way, you could
call this serendipity.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.830
But don't expect to just wake
up in that eureka moment.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.430
I think these connections build over time

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:38.590
so it's important for you to
be consistent in your research,

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.970
and in your designing,
and in your thinking.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:44.750
And step number six, is get feedback.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:47.730
This step is so important.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.230
You can't wait 'til
your product is perfect

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:51.680
to show it to the world.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.720
You have to show them your progress,

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.590
try to explain them the idea,

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.210
and get critiqued,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.350
whether it's your friends or your family,

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.040
and adjust your idea, based on that.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.770
Put your ego aside.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.360
And then number seven, you iterate.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:13.520
You repeat processes three to six

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.450
over, and over, and
over, and over, and over,

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.150
and over, and over, and over, and.

